export default "Cycle syncing as a practice has grown significantly in the past decade, but most period apps haven't kept up. \"Cycle syncing support\" in many apps means: we show you which phase you're in and write articles about it.\n\nReal cycle syncing support requires more.\n\n## What Cycle Syncing Apps Need to Do\n\n**Accurate phase identification:** Cycle phase should be based on when you actually ovulate, not on a fixed window calculated from average cycle length. A person with a 30-day cycle and late ovulation (Day 17) is in the follicular phase on Day 14; Flo's standard algorithm would say they're in the ovulatory phase. This matters because phase-appropriate recommendations are only useful if the phase identification is correct.\n\n**Phase-aware logging:** Observations logged on a specific day should be associated with the cycle phase active on that day. After 3+ cycles, this creates a personal record: \"What is my typical energy in the follicular phase? What is my typical sleep quality in the late luteal phase?\"\n\n**Actionable phase content:** Food and exercise recommendations adapted to phase (even if the user has to interpret and modify them).\n\n**Pattern retrospection:** The ability to look back at 3+ cycles and see how your logged symptoms map to phases, identifying your personal version of the cycle syncing patterns.\n\n## App Comparison\n\n### MyFlo (28)\n\n**What it does well:** Built specifically for cycle syncing by Alisa Vitti. Provides specific food, exercise, sex, and work recommendations by phase. Clear phase display. The most complete implementation of Vitti's cycle syncing framework available in an app.\n\n**Limitations:** Phase identification is based on period history, not ovulation tracking. If your cycles are irregular, phase windows won't be accurate. The prescriptive content doesn't adapt to your personal logged data, it gives you the protocol regardless of what your symptoms show.\n\n**Privacy:** US-based cloud app, account required.\n\n### Clue\n\n**What it does well:** Phase display is prominent in the UI. Phase-tagging of logged symptoms allows retrospective correlation. Handles irregular cycles better than most apps. Good export for analysis. German company, GDPR.\n\n**Limitations:** Cycle syncing is not the product focus, it's a secondary feature. Phase-specific content is limited compared to MyFlo. Phase identification based on period average, not ovulation confirmation.\n\n**Privacy:** German company, GDPR. Better than US apps.\n\n### Flo\n\n**What it does well:** Phase identification with health article content by phase. Large symptom logging library. Doctor reports for clinical sharing.\n\n**Limitations:** Phase identification is based on average cycle length. Cycle syncing content is generic and not adapted to your personal logged data. US cloud, account required.\n\n**Privacy:** US cloud. Improved post-2021 FTC action.\n\n### Floriva\n\n**What it does well:** Daily logging and on-device pattern review. Users can build their own phase-pattern awareness from their own data instead of following a fixed protocol. Check current Floriva onboarding for exact field and review options.\n\n**Limitations:** No prescriptive cycle syncing content, the app doesn't tell you what to eat on which days. Users must interpret their own data and apply cycle syncing principles themselves.\n\n**Privacy:** Local-first, no account. Highest architecture-based privacy.\n\n## Feature Comparison\n\n| Feature | MyFlo (28) | Clue | Flo | Floriva |\n|---------|-----------|------|-----|---------|\n| Phase identification | Period average | Period average | Period average | Review current app options |\n| Phase-aware symptom logging | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Phase-specific food/exercise guidance | Full protocol | Articles | Articles | None (data-only) |\n| Retrospective phase pattern analysis | Limited | ✓ | Limited | ✓ |\n| Irregular cycle support | Limited | Good | Moderate | Full |\n| Privacy | US cloud | EU cloud | US cloud | Local-first |\n\n## The Phase Identification Problem\n\nThe most important variable for cycle syncing accuracy is phase identification quality. Many apps base phase identification on period history averages. For a person with a consistent 28-day cycle, this is acceptable. For anyone with cycle-to-cycle variation of 3+ days, the phase windows may shift by the same amount.\n\nThis matters because the phase-specific recommendations in cycle syncing protocols, \"eat cruciferous vegetables in your follicular phase\", assume you're actually in the follicular phase. If your follicular phase is longer than average and you're still in it when the app says you're ovulatory, the content is misaligned.\n\nDirect ovulation tracking (BBT or LH strips) identifies phase boundaries from biological data, not statistical averages.\n\n## Who Each Is Right For\n\n**MyFlo:** Users who want a structured cycle syncing protocol with specific daily recommendations and are comfortable with the prescriptive content.\n\n**Clue:** Users who want phase tracking as part of a well-rounded cycle app with good irregular cycle support and GDPR protections.\n\n**Floriva:** Users who want privacy-first daily logging and are interested in building personal phase-pattern awareness rather than following a prescribed protocol.\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nCycle syncing in Floriva is evidence-informed and self-directed: you log how you feel, review your own notes, and notice what patterns emerge over 3+ cycles. This takes longer than following a protocol but can produce knowledge that fits your body better than a population average.";
